  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHigh pressure races across New England today, exiting to the \nsoutheast this evening. The timing will bring one more dry and \nmild day today before conditions turn more damp and cold into \nthe weekend.\n\nRain remains on schedule for Friday, pushing across the area \nwest to east in the morning. This will be associated with low \npressure that most model guidance agrees will pass along or just\nNW of the US/CAN border. The positively tilted trough across \nthe Great Lakes will ensure the low is progressive, pushing into\nthe Canadian Maritimes shortly after midnight Friday night.\n\nRain continues to be the primary precip type for the forecast \narea as the system passes to the north. There is the chance for \nsome flurries to mix in across the higher western ME mountains \nearly Friday morning, but this should quickly transition to rain\nfor the day. Ensemble IVT values are impressive, albeit \nbriefly, Friday afternoon. Greater than 750 kg/m/s is likely \nper both the GEFS and ENS. Combined with the jet dynamics and \nPWATs that near the 90th percentile for early/mid Oct, can't \nrule out a period of moderate to heavy rainfall rates. The \ntricky part is location, as global models poorly represent how \nrain transits the region as far as intensity goes. It is also \npossible a dry slot bisects periods of rain or showers.\n\nFor now this seems like the better solution given QPF totals\nthat have only slightly increased through the week, totaling\n0.5 to 1 inch. Without better instability to take advantage of \nthe plume of moisture, precip may be focused along features \nassociated with the low (fronts, fgen, etc). A better grasp on \nprecip presentation will come with the short term guidance \nwindow arriving.\n\nIt then becomes more showery Friday night with cold air moving\nin allowing for a transition to snow showers in the mountains.\nAs temperatures continue to cool into Saturday morning, snow\nwill likely make it to the valley floors across the north.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nStrong cold air advection arrives on Saturday with plummeting \ntemperatures. Very confident we will see 850 mb temperatures drop to \n-7 to -9 C. Even with mixed low-levels, this translates to surface \ntemperatures only in the 40s-near 50 for the coastal plain, with 30s \nin the higher terrain. Combined with strong winds during the day on \nSaturday, wind chills will be in the 20s-30s regionwide! Those with \noutdoor plans this weekend should dress for winter conditions. \nConditions will be potentially hazardous for hikers in high terrain.\n\nStrong northwest winds, combined with lingering boundary layer \nmoisture and steep low-level lapse rates, will provide a setup for \nupslope snow showers in the mountains. A few flakes are possible in \nthe foothills too.\n\nThe other headline will be strong post frontal winds Saturday. Wind \nspeeds at 850 mb are on the order of 30-40 kts. Mixing should be \nrather deep, so despite not particularly intense wind fields aloft, \nshould see a good bit of this reach the surface, especially in the \nhigher terrain. Gusts to 30 mph can be expected region-wide with \ngusts to 40 mph in the foothills, and much stronger winds above \ntreeline.\n\nThe weather turns far more tranquil from Sunday onward. After a very \ncold Saturday night (with lows bottoming out in the 20s-low 30s \nregionwide), temperatures start a steady moderating trend. High \npressure remains in control of the weather pattern for at least \nearly to midweek next week, but models are indicating that low \npressure likely begins to organize to our south during this \ntimeframe, and will be worth monitoring through the forecast period. \nTemperatures look to be seasonable to potentially mild for this \nstretch with daytime 50s-60s.",
